<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezzelino III da Romano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1194">1194</a>–<a href="/years/1259">1259</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ezzelino III da Romano was a Ghibelline lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-treviso">March of Treviso</a> and an ally of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. From the 1220s to the 1250s he dominated Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, ruling with a cruelty that made him a byword for tyranny. A papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> was proclaimed against him, and he was wounded and captured at Cassano in 1259, dying soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano.jpg" alt="Ezzelino III da Romano" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ezzelino_III_da_Romano_(by_Cristofano_dell%27Altissimo).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ezzelino was born on 25 April 1194 at Tombolo, the son of Ezzelino II da Romano, ruler of Bassano del Grappa and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, and of Adelaide, of the Alberti di Mangona, a family of Tuscan counts. At the age of four he was sent as a hostage to Verona. In 1213 he took part in the siege of the castle of Este; according to the chronicler Rolandino of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> he already showed a keen interest in siegecraft and a hatred of the Este that lasted all his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When his father retired to a monastery in 1223, his brother Alberico received the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> and Ezzelino those around Treviso. In 1226 Ezzelino intervened in a factional struggle in Verona and became its podestà. The city mattered because whoever held it could block the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a> and cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> off from German reinforcements in his war with the Second Lombard League. The brothers at first favoured the League, but changed sides when it backed their rivals, the Este and the San Bonifacio, and allied with Frederick in 1232.</p><p>When the emperor came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-treviso">March of Treviso</a> in 1236, he and Ezzelino took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and Treviso surrendered early in 1237. Ezzelino married Frederick's natural daughter Selvaggia, fought in the imperial victory at Cortenuova in 1237 and was named imperial viceroy for the March in 1239. He went on to seize Treviso, Belluno and Feltre, becoming lord of the lands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a> and the Oglio, and used torture against enemies and suspected plotters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> excommunicated him in 1254.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1258 Ezzelino launched a broad Ghibelline offensive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> with Oberto Pallavicino of Cremona. After a failed attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> he was wounded by an arrow and retreated, and was taken near Bergamo. He died through deliberate self-neglect in the castle of Soncino, near Cremona. In the following year Alberico, his wife and all their children were tortured and put to death, and the Romano family died out.</p><p>Much of what is known of Ezzelino comes from a hostile literary tradition that grew over the centuries. Rolandino's partisan Chronicle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-treviso">Trevisan March</a> followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> under his rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertino-mussato">Albertino Mussato</a>'s Latin play Ecerinis portrayed him as the son of the Devil, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> placed him in the seventh circle of Hell among the violent against their neighbours. His example of seizing power by sheer ruthlessness was not lost on the tyrants of late medieval and Renaissance Italy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ezzelino III da Romano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The judge Lovato Lovati studied the metres of Seneca’s tragedies, and Albertino Mussato wrote a Latin tragedy in Seneca’s manner, the Ecerinis, on the tyranny of <b>Ezzelino III da Romano</b>, for which Padua crowned him with laurel in 1315.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1237 to 1256 the city suffered under <b>Ezzelino III da Romano</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-padua">Commune of Padua</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ezzelino III da Romano's</b> brother Alberico it suffered harsh rule until 1259.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-treviso">Commune of Treviso</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1220s the city was dominated by <b>Ezzelino III da Romano</b>, a Ghibelline ally of Frederick II whose harsh rule over Verona, Vicenza and Padua made him a byword for tyranny.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-verona">Commune of Verona</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1230s the city came under <b>Ezzelino III da Romano</b>, and after his fall Padua controlled Vicenza from 1266.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-vicenza">Commune of Vicenza</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Ezzelino III da Romano</b> died in 1259, the Commune of Verona chose Mastino I della Scala as its leader, and by 1262 he was effectively lord.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Soncino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=1236" aria-label="Padua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4832 562 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ezzelino III da Romano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 832h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 832h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=1236">What was here in 1236? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
